  "notes": "The last node of the 2000s and the hinge into 2010s club-pop: 118–130 straight, four-on-the-floor with claps on 2&4, so the kick sits on all four beats with `syncopation` at 0.04. The harmony is documented as the era's defining cadence — a rising B-flat–C–Dm cycle, VI–VII–i, \"the 'aeolian cadence' that dominates late-2000s pop; **no dominant chord**\" — so the families are the rising cycle and its rotation, `maxChords` is 3, and no V appears anywhere in the block. The drum block is the entry's \"minimalist 'bitpop' programmed kit\" and the two facts that make it unusual are both authored: \"handclaps as the main top layer\" and \"**8-bit blip percussion instead of hats**\", which is why `hihat.continuous` is false at a fill density of 0.08 while the perc block runs at 4–7 with snap, perc and a riser for the \"filtered white-noise riser into each chorus\". The bass is \"square/saw synth bass on roots in 8ths, side-chained under the kick\" with the sub dropping out during the pre-chorus, so `dropOutBeats` is high. The topline is documented as \"rapped/talked on 2–3 pitches\" in the verse against a sung chorus across D3–D5, which is the wide density range over `motifPitchCount` 2–5 — the floor of 2 is the entry's own verse count and D3–D5 is `melody.register`. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are the drum probabilities, the melody density split and the `motifPitchCount` ceiling of 5: the entry counts pitches for the verse only and gives the sung chorus no count, so the top of that range is this author's reading; \"very high in verse, low in chorus\" is a direction and 4–12 is this author's reading of it.",
